From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 17:13:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: qca8k: add an 802.1Q VLAN-based tag driver

The Atheros header that tag_qca puts in front of the ethertype is
understood by the switch and by tag_qca alone. Anything that sits between
the switch and the CPU and wants to parse the frame - a hardware
accelerator on the conduit's MAC, an offload engine that only knows
802.1Q - drops it, so on such a SoC DSA user ports and the accelerator
exclude each other.

Add a tag driver built on the tag_8021q framework: the switch tells the
CPU where a frame came from with a VLAN tag it inserts itself, and the
CPU says where a frame goes the same way. The conduit then carries plain
802.1Q, and the switch stays a DSA switch with phylink, LEDs, MIB
counters and bridge offload as before.

The qca8k side selects the mode through change_tag_protocol, so the
default stays the Atheros header and the VLAN tagger is chosen from the
device tree (dsa-tag-protocol = "qca-8021q" on the CPU port) or at
runtime through /sys/class/net/<conduit>/dsa/tagging. In that mode the
header is turned off on the CPU ports, every port is VLAN-aware with the
tag_8021q VID as PVID, bridge join and leave swap the standalone VID for
the bridge's, FDB and MDB entries the bridge hands over without a VID go
into the tag_8021q VID where the lookups happen, and management over
Ethernet is left alone since it rides on the header.

An 802.1Q upper of a standalone port (wan.35, an ISP that wants its
traffic tagged) is carried as it is. The user ports advertise
NETIF_F_HW_VLAN_CTAG_FILTER in this mode, which is what makes the core
ask the switch for the VLAN when the upper is created: the port and the
CPU port become tagged members of it, one such VLAN belongs to one port.
The tagger sends a frame that already carries the upper's tag with that
tag alone instead of stacking the port's VID under it, and on receive a
VLAN outside the tag_8021q range goes to the port that has the upper,
where the kernel's VLAN demux takes over.

A VLAN-aware bridge works the way it does on sja1105: its VLANs go into
the switch as they are, PVID and untagged included, the CPU port is a
tagged member of each, and a frame received in one of them is handed to
a port of that bridge which is in the VLAN, the tag left on for the
bridge to do its own VLAN handling. Which of the bridge's ports it came
from is not known - the same imprecise receive as for a VLAN-unaware
bridge, where the switch forwards between its own ports anyway. The
port keeps two PVIDs, the tag_8021q one and the bridge's, and
vlan_filtering picks which the register holds. One rule follows from
the VID being all the tagger has: a VID outside the tag_8021q range
names one thing on the switch, the VLAN of one VLAN-aware bridge or an
802.1Q upper of one port, and port_vlan_add and port_prechangeupper
refuse the rest.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/include/net/dsa.h
+++ b/include/net/dsa.h
@@ -57,6 +57,7 @@ struct tc_action;
 #define DSA_TAG_PROTO_BRCM_LEGACY_FCS_VALUE	29
 #define DSA_TAG_PROTO_MXL862_VALUE		30
 #define DSA_TAG_PROTO_MXL862_8021Q_VALUE	31
+#define DSA_TAG_PROTO_QCA_8021Q_VALUE		34
 
 enum dsa_tag_protocol {
 	DSA_TAG_PROTO_NONE		= DSA_TAG_PROTO_NONE_VALUE,
@@ -91,6 +92,7 @@ enum dsa_tag_protocol {
 	DSA_TAG_PROTO_VSC73XX_8021Q	= DSA_TAG_PROTO_VSC73XX_8021Q_VALUE,
 	DSA_TAG_PROTO_MXL862		= DSA_TAG_PROTO_MXL862_VALUE,
 	DSA_TAG_PROTO_MXL862_8021Q	= DSA_TAG_PROTO_MXL862_8021Q_VALUE,
+	DSA_TAG_PROTO_QCA_8021Q		= DSA_TAG_PROTO_QCA_8021Q_VALUE,
 };
 
 struct dsa_switch;
--- a/net/dsa/Kconfig
+++ b/net/dsa/Kconfig
@@ -151,6 +151,14 @@ config NET_DSA_TAG_QCA
 	  Say Y or M if you want to enable support for tagging frames for
 	  the Qualcomm Atheros QCA8K switches.
 
+config NET_DSA_TAG_QCA_8021Q
+	tristate "Tag driver for Qualcomm Atheros QCA8K switches, using VLAN"
+	help
+	  Say Y or M if you want to enable support for tagging frames for
+	  the Qualcomm Atheros QCA8K switches using 802.1Q VLAN tags instead
+	  of the 2-byte Atheros header. The conduit then sees plain VLAN
+	  frames, which anything in front of the CPU can parse.
+
